# Rubidium — Metaverse Browser Application

> **This file is the project's shared knowledge base.** Any developer or AI assistant who clones this repository inherits this file as their starting context. It is not a sequential log — it does not record session history or append dated entries. It describes what the project *is right now*: architecture, codebase structure, dependencies, build decisions and their rationale, conventions, and roadmap status. Update sections in place as the project evolves. Session-level notes belong in session log files, not here.

## Overview

**Rubidium** is the metaverse browser application — the single `.exe` the user runs. Analogous to Chromium in the web browser world. It is built on top of the **Sneeze** engine, which provides the rendering, compute, sandboxing, and networking abstractions. Rubidium is responsible for everything the user directly interacts with: the application window, chrome (URL bar, menus, toolbars), and `main()`. All C++ application code lives in `namespace RUBIDIUM`.

The GitHub repository is **public**. Source files remain proprietary: every `.h` / `.cpp` carries `// Copyright 2026 Metaversal Corporation. All rights reserved.` There is no `LICENSE` file and no grant to modify or redistribute. The `NOTICE` file credits Sneeze (Apache 2.0) and SDL3 (zlib).

### Relationship to Artemis

**Rubidium** is the public upstream application core (Chromium / VS Code in this split). Metaversal's **Artemis** is a private branded derivative (Edge / Cursor): different product strings, installer identity, and extra features. Artemis keeps `RUBIDIUM::` and the shared file layout so it can periodically `git fetch` this repository and `git merge`. Shared work lands here first. Artemis-only work is never merged back.

**Branding overlay (`branding/`):** Product identity is isolated so Artemis does not fork chrome/build files. The overlay is:

| File | Who reads it |
|------|----------------|
| `branding/Product.cmake` | CMake (`include`), generates `Brand.h` via `configure_file`. Source of truth for C++ and CPack. |
| `branding/Brand.h.in` | Template. CMake writes `Brand.h` into the build dir; MSVC PreBuildEvent writes `Brand.h` into `$(IntDir)` via `branding/Generate-BrandHeader.ps1`. |
| `branding/Brand.props` | Hand-maintained MSVC projects (`TargetName`). Must stay in sync with `Product.cmake` (`PRODUCT_NAME` / `PRODUCT_NAME_SETUP`). |

Call sites use `PRODUCT_NAME`, `PRODUCT_HOME_URL`, `PRODUCT_CDN_URL`, `PRODUCT_APPDATA_DIR`, `PRODUCT_WINDOW_CLASS`, `PRODUCT_MUTEX`, `OUTPUT_NAME`, CPack/NSIS strings, and macOS bundle id — not string literals. CMake target names stay `Rubidium` / `RubidiumSetup`; only the on-disk binary name changes (`OUTPUT_NAME` / `$(ProductName)`).

Artemis replaces `branding/Product.cmake` and `branding/Brand.props` (and logos/LICENSE/README as needed). It does **not** rename `namespace RUBIDIUM`, include guards, or source paths.

**Not in the hook (known overlay exceptions):** Android Java package `com.rp1.Rubidium` and the JNI export `Java_com_rp1_Rubidium_MainActivity_*` (renaming those requires a matching Java folder). `pkg/macos/Rubidium.entitlements` filename. README/NOTICE/LICENSE prose.

`-DRUBIDIUM_CDN_URL=` still overrides the CDN at CMake configure time; that value is written into generated `Brand.h` as `PRODUCT_CDN_URL`.

Both Rubidium and Sneeze are part of the **Open Metaverse Browser Initiative (OMBI)**, an organizational unit under the Metaverse Standards Forum. OMBI's mission is to define and build a reference implementation of the Open Metaverse Browser (OMB). Sneeze is the engine (open source, Apache 2.0); Rubidium is the application (proprietary).

### Relationship to Sneeze

- **Sneeze** is the Metaverse Browser Engine (MBE) — a standalone, reusable static library (`.lib`). It owns all third-party engine dependencies (Wasmtime, SPIRV-Tools, OpenXR, curl, RmlUi, ANARI, glslang) and the engine modules that wrap them. Another team could build a different metaverse browser on top of Sneeze without touching Rubidium.
- **Rubidium** consumes Sneeze via CMake `add_subdirectory`. Edit a `.cpp` in either repo, hit build — CMake detects the change and rebuilds the right targets. No commit/push/fetch cycle required.
- **Standalone Sneeze build is NOT consumed by Rubidium.** Running `Sneeze/scripts/build-<platform>` produces `Sneeze/builds/<plat>/install/<cfg>/lib/Sneeze.lib`. Building Rubidium produces `Sneeze.lib` from Sneeze's sources via `add_subdirectory` at the **same location** (`Sneeze/builds/<plat>/install/<cfg>/lib/Sneeze.lib`) — all Sneeze build activity is routed into Sneeze's own `builds/` folder, never under Rubidium. Standalone and inline builds share the same output path and overwrite each other, which is acceptable since you're either building from one or the other, not both simultaneously. The standalone build exists only so Sneeze can be tested/built on its own. For day-to-day Rubidium work, running Sneeze's build script is **optional** — Rubidium's build script alone produces a complete `Rubidium.exe` with its own inline-built `Sneeze.lib`. What Rubidium **does** require from the Sneeze repo is Sneeze's **prebuilt deps tree** at `Sneeze/deps/builds/<plat>/<cfg>/libs/` (Wasmtime, ANARI, SPIRV-Tools, curl, etc.) — those are consumed by both the standalone and inline Sneeze builds.
- **SDL3** belongs to Rubidium, not Sneeze. The engine is embeddable without owning windowing. The application (Rubidium) creates the window and passes raw input deltas to the engine.
- **SNEEZE_DIR** cache variable defaults to `../Sneeze`. Override with `-DSNEEZE_DIR=<path>` for non-standard layouts or CI.
- The hand-maintained `msvc/Rubidium.sln` contains three projects — the Rubidium executable, the Sneeze static library, and the Sneeze test suite — one IDE instance, full navigation and debugging across both repos. The CMake-generated `.sln` under `builds/` also contains both targets but is not the primary dev artifact.

**Prerequisite:** Sneeze's third-party deps must be built first via its deps tree (`Sneeze/deps/`). Rubidium doesn't rebuild those — it needs them to exist at `Sneeze/deps/builds/<platform>/<config>/libs/`.

## Architecture Reference

The OMB architecture is fully documented in `OMB_Architecture_4.md` (the authoritative architecture document, version 4, 2026 Q2, maintained outside both repos). This document covers the MBE (Metaverse Browser Engine), its core abstractions, the SOM, rendering engine, UI toolkit, identity, avatars, spatial audio, and the content provider ecosystem.

### MBE Core Abstractions (Sneeze-side, consumed by Rubidium)

The MBE relies on six industry-adopted open standards that abstract the underlying hardware:

- **ANARI** — rendering abstraction (scene description, not GPU calls)
- **SPIR-V Compute via ANARI** — browser-internal GPU compute
- **SPIR-V** — GPU interchange format for graphics and compute shaders
- **OpenXR** — device I/O abstraction for VR/AR
- **WASM** — sandboxed execution for third-party service logic
- **SMAP** (Service Model Access Protocol) — fabric connectivity and service driver layer

### Core Concepts

- **Spatial Fabric** — a collection of services that together define a spatial environment
- **Service** — a discrete unit of functionality within a spatial fabric
- **SOM** (Scene Object Model) — the browser's internal scene graph (like the DOM for 3D)
- **Proximity** — the primary discovery and connection mechanism, replacing URLs

