### main.cpp and APP — Application Entry and Lifecycle

`main.cpp` is a thin entry point: obtains `RUBIDIUM::APPNATIVE::GetInstance()` and calls `Run()`. On Windows, it uses `WinMain` with a temporary `AllocConsole()` for stdout/stderr. On other platforms, it uses standard `main()`.

**Class hierarchy:** `APP` (abstract base, `App.h`/`App.cpp`) → `APPNATIVE` (Win32 derived, `App_Win32.h`/`App_Win32.cpp`). Other platforms will have their own `APPNATIVE` derivations.

**`APP` base class** (`src/shell/App.cpp`): constructed with platform-resolved app data path + ILOGGER backend. Resolves `Metaversal/Rubidium/` directories, loads `settings.json` with `merge_patch` against defaults (so all keys the runtime reads always exist — prevents crashes from older/incomplete settings files). Destructor writes settings to disk. Owns `m_pLogger`, `m_sFontFamily`, `m_jSettings`, `m_sHomePath`, `m_mapSDLWindow`. Implements `SNEEZE::IENGINE` (`sAppDataPath()`, `sRenderer()` = `"halogen"`, `Log()`). Provides SDL window registry (`SDLWindow_Register`/`Unregister`/`Translate`).

**`APPNATIVE::Impl::Run()`** (Win32, `src/shell/App_Win32.cpp`) is the platform startup sequence:

- Sets DPI awareness (`DPI_AWARENESS_CONTEXT_PER_MONITOR_AWARE_V2`)
- Registers window class (`RubidiumFrameClass`) and initializes common controls
- Reads log level from settings via `m_pApp->SettingToJSON()["logger"]["level"]`, restores via `m_pApp->Logger()->LogLevel()`
- Creates Sneeze engine (`new SNEEZE::ENGINE(m_pApp)` — `APP` implements `IENGINE`)
- Resolves platform font family (`WINUTILS::DefaultFontFamily`/`FontFilePath`), stores via `m_pApp->sFontFamily()`, calls `Rml::LoadFontFace`
- `SDL_Init(SDL_INIT_VIDEO)`
- Creates initial window via `Window_Create(nullptr, kSESSION_PERSISTENT)`
- Enters `EventLoop()`

**`APPNATIVE::Impl::EventLoop()`** — the main loop:

1. Drain Win32 message queue (`PeekMessageA` + accelerator dispatch to active window)
2. Drain SDL event queue: `while (SDL_PollEvent(&Event)) m_pApp->SDLWindow_Translate(Event)` — dispatches each event to the registered `ISDLWINDOW` by window ID
3. Call `pAppFrame->ProcessInput()` for ALL AppFrames (not just active — ensures scroll deltas reach unfocused viewports)
4. `MsgWaitForMultipleObjects(0, nullptr, FALSE, INFINITE, QS_ALLINPUT)` — blocks until next input arrives

**Shutdown:** last window's `Window_OnDestroy` calls `PostQuitMessage(0)`, loop exits, `SNEEZE::ENGINE` deleted, `APPNATIVE` destructor calls `DestroyLogger()` + deletes `m_pILogger`, then `~APP()` writes settings to disk.

**SDL Event Dispatch Architecture:** SDL events are polled exactly once per frame in the main loop. `APP::SDLWindow_Translate(SDL_Event&)` extracts the `windowID` from the event (switch on event type → appropriate union member), looks up `m_mapSDLWindow` (unordered_map, O(1)), and calls `HandleEvent()` on the matching `ISDLWINDOW`. Currently two implementors: `CANVAS` (input state accumulation) and `RMLUI_SDL` (RmlUi event routing + immediate render). This eliminates competing `SDL_PollEvent` loops and guarantees no event is consumed by the wrong window.

Engine types are referenced with fully qualified `SNEEZE::CORE::`, `SNEEZE::renderer::`, `SNEEZE::view::` prefixes. No `using namespace SNEEZE;` — keep the boundary visible.

### Design Principles (Rubidium-Specific)

- **Engine boundary is sacred.** Rubidium never includes Sneeze's internal headers directly. It uses the public module interfaces. The engine has no SDL3 dependency — Rubidium passes raw input deltas.
- **Namespace everything.** All Rubidium code in `RUBIDIUM::` (no module sub-namespaces — classes live directly in the top-level namespace). Sneeze types are always fully qualified (`SNEEZE::CORE::`, `SNEEZE::renderer::`, etc.).
- **Chrome is Rubidium's domain.** URL bar, menus, toolbars, browser settings — all Rubidium. RmlUi (in Sneeze) handles in-world 3D UI; never the browser chrome.

### Logging Architecture

Two logging paths exist — one for Rubidium code, one for Sneeze code — both producing identical formatted output.

**Rubidium-side:** `LOGGER*` is owned by `APP` (created at construction) and passed to `APPFRAME` instances via their constructor. All Rubidium modules call `m_pLogger->Log(LOGGER::kLOGLEVEL_Info, "MODULE", "message")`. The `LOGGER` class queues messages on a background thread, formats them with timestamp + level + module tag, and dispatches to `ILOGGER_NATIVE::onMessage()` (which calls `std::printf` on Windows, `SDL_Log` on other platforms). The formatter does NOT append `\n` — the output backend owns line termination.

**Sneeze-side:** No global logger. Every Sneeze module receives a `CORE::SNEEZE*` pointer (stored as `m_pSneeze`) via its constructor or `Initialize()` method. Modules call `m_pSneeze->Log(CORE::SNEEZE_LISTENER::kLOGLEVEL_Info, "MODULE", "message")`. `SNEEZE::Log()` delegates to the `ISNEEZE` interface, which `APP` implements by forwarding to `m_pLogger->Log()`. This keeps the engine free of globals while providing structured logging through the host interface.

**Log levels:** `kLOGLEVEL_Trace` (teardown phases, FPS telemetry, metronome stats, WASM instance lifecycle), `kLOGLEVEL_Info` (module initialization, version banners, startup milestones), `kLOGLEVEL_Warning` (XR runtime not detected, non-fatal library load failures), `kLOGLEVEL_Error` (initialization failures, compile errors, runtime errors), `kLOGLEVEL_Off` (suppresses all log output).

**User-configurable log level:** The ellipsis menu contains a "Log Level" submenu with radio-checked items (Trace, Info, Warning, Error, Off — separator before Off). Selecting an item calls `m_pLogger->LogLevel(level)` immediately and writes the level as a lowercase string (`"trace"`, `"info"`, `"warning"`, `"error"`, `"off"`) to `settings.json` at `logger.level`. On startup, `APP::Run()` reads `logger.level` from settings and restores it (defaults to `"info"` if the key is missing, covering existing settings files). The checkmark updates each time the menu opens via `UpdateLogLevelMenu()` called from `ShowEllipsisMenu()`.

**MSF_FILE special case:** `MSF_FILE` is a standalone utility class used by both the engine and CLI tools (`SignMsf`). Its constructor takes an optional `CORE::SNEEZE* pSneeze = nullptr`. When non-null, errors route through `m_pSneeze->Log()`. When null (CLI tool context), errors fall back to `std::fprintf(stderr, ...)`.

**No `printf` / `fprintf` in module code.** All logging goes through the logger. The only remaining `std::printf` is inside `ILOGGER_NATIVE::onMessage()` (the output backend itself). `std::snprintf` for buffer formatting (e.g., FPS string, SHA hex) is acceptable — it's writing to a buffer, not to stdout.

### Coding Conventions (C++)

These conventions match Sneeze and carry over from the existing JavaScript codebase:

- **3-space indentation.** Not 2, not 4, never tabs.
- **Allman brace style.** Opening `{` on its own line.
- **Hungarian notation.** All variables carry type prefixes: `p` pointer/object, `d` double/float, `n` number, `s` string, `b` bool, `a` array, `tm` time, `fn` function.
- **No abbreviated variable names.** Local and member variables must be descriptive: `nWidth` not `nW`, `nHeight` not `nH`, `nTab` not `n`, `hTab` not `h`, `Rect` not `rc`, `WindowPlacement` not `wp`, `WndClassExW` not `wc`, `hMonitor` not `hMon`, `InitCommonControls` not `icc`. Win32 struct-type locals use the type name directly (e.g., `RECT Rect`, `WINDOWPLACEMENT WindowPlacement`, `WNDCLASSEXW WndClassExW`).
- **Types, classes, constants, namespaces: ALL CAPS.** `CANVAS`, `CELESTIAL`, `ORBIT`, `RMCOBJECT`, `namespace RUBIDIUM`, `namespace SNEEZE`.
- **Functions: Capital first letter.** `Initialize()`, `PollEvents()`, `ComputeOrbital()`.
- **Getter/setter naming: no `Get` prefix, overloaded setters.** Getters drop the `Get` prefix: `NativeWindow()` not `GetNativeWindow()`, `Width()` not `GetWidth()`, `JSON()` not `GetJSON()`, `LogLevel()` not `GetLogLevel()`. Setters use the same name as the getter, overloaded by parameter: `UrlText()` (getter) / `UrlText(const std::string&)` (setter), `LogLevel()` (getter) / `LogLevel(eLOGLEVEL)` (setter). Exception: `GetInstance()` on singletons is kept as-is (it's a factory/accessor, not a property getter).
- **Single return at end.** One `return` statement, last line of function. No early returns — use inverted guard conditions or wrap the body in `if` blocks to reach a single exit point.
- **Cross-language invariance.** Class names and algorithms must remain identical across JS and C++ implementations.
- **Proprietary copyright header** on every `.h` and `.cpp` file: `// Copyright 2026 Metaversal Corporation. All rights reserved.`
- **Traditional include guards** on every `.h` file: `#ifndef RUBIDIUM_MODULE_FILENAME_H` / `#define RUBIDIUM_MODULE_FILENAME_H` / `#endif`.

### Dependencies

Rubidium has two direct dependencies:

| Dependency | Version | Source | Purpose | Owner |
|------------|---------|--------|---------|-------|
| **Sneeze** | (from `VERSION`) | `../Sneeze` (or `SNEEZE_DIR`) | Engine library — rendering, compute, sandboxing, networking | Compiled inline via `add_subdirectory` |
| **SDL3** | 3.4.2 | [libsdl-org/SDL](https://github.com/libsdl-org/SDL) | Windowing, input, framebuffer display (static linked) | Built by Rubidium deps tree (`deps/sdl3.cmake`) |

All of Sneeze's transitive dependencies (ANARI, Wasmtime, SPIRV-Tools, OpenXR, curl, RmlUi, glslang, nlohmann/json) propagate automatically through CMake PUBLIC link declarations. Rubidium never configures or finds those — Sneeze handles them. The Updater and GenerateManifest build tool use nlohmann/json for JSON parsing.

**Build-time prerequisites (installed by the developer):**

| Tool | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| CMake 3.20+ | Build system generator |
| C/C++ compiler (C++17) | MSVC 2022, GCC, or Clang |
| Git | Cloning SDL3 |
| NSIS (Windows, packaging only) | Generates Windows installer (no plugins required) |

Rust and Python are NOT needed for Rubidium — those are Sneeze build-time deps only.

### Build System

Rubidium has **two fully independent CMake trees**, mirroring Sneeze's architecture. There is **no top-level `CMakeLists.txt`** — the scripts in `scripts/` are the only glue.

- **`deps/CMakeLists.txt`** — Standalone deps project. `cmake -S deps` builds SDL3. Never references `src/`. Derives `RUBIDIUM_CONFIG`, `RUBIDIUM_PLATFORM`, `RUBIDIUM_DEP_REPO`, `LIBS_DIR` if not passed explicitly.
- **`deps/sdl3.cmake`** — Single `ExternalProject_Add` for SDL3. Clones into `${RUBIDIUM_DEP_REPO}/SDL3/` (shared across configs), builds + installs under `${LIBS_DIR}/SDL3/`.
- **`src/CMakeLists.txt`** — Standalone Rubidium project. `cmake -S src`. Finds SDL3 under `${RUBIDIUM_LIBS_DIR}/SDL3/install/`. Compiles Sneeze inline via `add_subdirectory("${SNEEZE_DIR}/src" "${SNEEZE_DIR}/builds/${RUBIDIUM_PLATFORM}/build")` — sets `LIBS_DIR = SNEEZE_LIBS_DIR` before the call so Sneeze finds its own deps, and sets `SNEEZE_BUILD_ROOT = ${SNEEZE_DIR}/builds/${RUBIDIUM_PLATFORM}` so all Sneeze build activity (intermediates and final outputs) stays under Sneeze's own folder. Forces Rubidium output to `${RUBIDIUM_BUILD_ROOT}/install/$<LOWER_CASE:$<CONFIG>>/bin` via generator-expression-backed output directories (`CMAKE_RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY`, `CMAKE_LIBRARY_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY`, `CMAKE_ARCHIVE_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY`) — a single multi-config CMake tree emits both Debug and Release into per-config install siblings. SDL3 imported targets are dualized (manual `IMPORTED_LOCATION_DEBUG`/`_RELEASE` set by walking `_rubidium_dualize_target` — same pattern Sneeze uses for its own deps). `CMAKE_CONFIGURATION_TYPES` is forced to `"Debug;Release"` **before** `project()` so VS/Xcode/Ninja-MC don't probe `MinSizeRel` / `RelWithDebInfo` (which have no deps trees on disk). Source files organized by module: `SHELL_SOURCES`/`SHELL_HEADERS` (per-platform via `if(WIN32)/else`), `CANVAS_SOURCES`, `ASTRO_SOURCES`, `UPDATER_SOURCES`, `LOGGER_SOURCES`. Defines the `Rubidium` executable target (with `rubidium.rc` on Windows for embedded icon), links `Sneeze`, `SDL3::SDL3-static`, and on Windows: `dwmapi`, `comctl32`, `uxtheme`. Post-build copies `anari_library_halogen.dll` and `wasmtime.dll` from `SNEEZE_LIBS_BASE_GEN` (config-aware form of `SNEEZE_LIBS_DIR`). Also defines the `RubidiumSetup` target, `GenerateManifest`, `package_register`/`package_promote` convenience targets. CPack output directory is per-config (`install/<config>/pkg/`) via a generated `CPackProjectConfig.cmake` that reads `CPACK_BUILD_CONFIG` at CPack time (generator expressions don't work in CPack vars).
- **`scripts/build-{windows.ps1,linux.sh,macos.sh}`** — Platform build drivers. Default (no flag): plain `cmake --build` (no dep checks, no configure). `-Deps`/`--deps`: builds deps only. `-All`/`--all`: deps flow, then `cmake -S src` configure + build. `-Fresh`/`--fresh`: **configure-only** — passes `cmake --fresh` (CMake 3.24+) to wipe `CMakeCache.txt` + `CMakeFiles/` and regenerate the project files, but does NOT build. To build afterward, run the script again with no flags. `-Fresh -Rebuild`/`--fresh --rebuild` composes: fresh configure + clean + build. Renamed from the earlier `-Configure` to avoid collision with `-Config Debug|Release`. `-All`'s reconfigure stays non-destructive. Stamp-cached: built deps are skipped on subsequent runs.
- **`-Rebuild` / `--rebuild` is a modifier, NOT a mode.** It composes with whatever target set is selected by the other flags — it does not pick a target set on its own. Matrix: `-Rebuild` alone → clean + rebuild **Rubidium, current config only**, via `cmake --build <build-dir> --target clean --config <cfg>` against the existing `builds/<platform>/build/` tree (preserves `CMakeCache.txt`, `CMakeFiles/`, and the generated `Rubidium.sln`/`.vcxproj` so Visual Studio keeps working), plus `rm -rf builds/<platform>/install/<cfg>/` for the current `-Config`. The OTHER config's intermediates (inside `build/`) and its `install/<other>/` are left alone. If the tree has never been configured, falls back to a full configure + build. `-Rebuild -Deps` → scrub + rebuild all deps (wipes `deps/builds/<platform>/<config>/`). `-Rebuild -Only <dep>` → scrub + rebuild one dep (its stamp, ExternalProject prefix, and `libs/<dep>/`). `-Rebuild -All` → scrub + rebuild deps, then Rubidium. Source clones in `deps/repos/` are never scrubbed. **HARD RULE:** the deps folder is only ever modified when `-Deps`, `-Only`, or `-All` is present — `-Rebuild` alone (or `-Fresh` alone) cannot touch a single bit inside `deps/`. This parallels the CMakeLists-level invariant that `deps/CMakeLists.txt` and `src/CMakeLists.txt` never reference each other's trees.
  - **Earlier buggy semantics (fixed).** Before the port from Sneeze (2026-04-20), Rubidium's `-Rebuild` was a MODE — the script's `$DepsMode` trigger included `$Rebuild`, so `-Rebuild` alone implied `-Deps` and scrubbed the dep root instead of the Rubidium tree. Fixed by removing `$Rebuild` from the mode trigger, adding `$RubidiumOutDir` scrub when `-Rebuild -and $RubidiumMode`, and wiring `$Reconfigure = [bool]($All -or $Fresh -or ($Rebuild -and $RubidiumMode))`. Same fix applied to `build-linux.sh` and `build-macos.sh`: `--rebuild` captured as a separate `REBUILD=1` flag instead of being lumped into `DEPS_FORWARD`, then forwarded to `build-deps.sh` only when `DEPS_MODE=1 && REBUILD=1`.
- **ExternalProject configure-stamp invalidation.** All scripts (including `build-deps.sh`) call `Invalidate-DepConfigure` / `invalidate_dep_configure` before each dep's build. Removes `$DepsBuildDir/<dep>-prefix/src/<dep>-stamp/<Config>/<dep>-configure`. ExternalProject_Add only re-runs its configure step if that stamp is missing; when a dep's configure succeeds but its build fails, the stamp stays and a later retry reuses cached CMAKE_ARGS even if `deps/<dep>.cmake` changed. Invalidating it forces the retry to pick up the current args. Matches Sneeze's behavior exactly.
- **`scripts/build-deps.sh`** — Shared bash helper for Linux/macOS dep iteration.

### Build Commands (Quick Reference)

```powershell
# --- Windows (PowerShell) ---
# THE day-to-day command -- compile + link Rubidium only (seconds)
.\scripts\build-windows.ps1                    # Release (default)
.\scripts\build-windows.ps1 -Config Debug      # Debug

# First-time setup: build SDL3 + configure + build Rubidium
.\scripts\build-windows.ps1 -All
.\scripts\build-windows.ps1 -All -Config Debug

# Refresh deps only (SDL3 changed upstream, rare)
.\scripts\build-windows.ps1 -Deps
.\scripts\build-windows.ps1 -Only sdl3 -Rebuild   # full-scrub one dep

# Run the application
builds\windows-x64\install\release\bin\Rubidium.exe
```

```bash
# --- Linux / macOS ---
./scripts/build-linux.sh                       # Release (default)
./scripts/build-linux.sh --config Debug        # Debug
./scripts/build-macos.sh                       # macOS Release
./scripts/build-linux.sh --all                 # First-time setup
./builds/linux-x64/install/release/bin/Rubidium # Run
```

```powershell
# --- Android phone (android-arm64, XR off, min SDK 26) ---
.\scripts\build-android.ps1 -All
.\scripts\build-android.ps1                    # incremental after the tree exists

# --- Quest 3 (quest-arm64, XR on, min SDK 29, immersive OpenXR) ---
.\scripts\build-android.ps1 -Quest -All
.\scripts\build-android.ps1 -Quest
# Sideload (debug keystore is regenerated each pack — uninstall first):
adb uninstall com.rp1.Rubidium
adb install builds\quest-arm64\install\release\pkg\rubidium-<ver>-quest-arm64-signed.apk
```

Phone and Quest are separate CMake trees. Do not FORCE `SNEEZE_ENABLE_XR` off on Android in CMake; the scripts pass OFF (phone) or ON (Quest). Quest does not create an SDL window — OpenXR owns presentation. GitHub `quest` / `apk-quest` jobs stay disabled until a donned-headset sideload is the normal path.

### CI / CD

Rubidium uses a split CI strategy:

| Platform | System | Config |
|---|---|---|
| Linux, macOS, iOS, Android, Quest | GitHub Actions (`.github/workflows/build.yml`) | Runs on every push to `main`, PRs, and manual dispatch. Downloads pre-built Sneeze libs from the Sneeze repo's CI artifacts. Builds SDL3 from source (cached). |
| Windows x64 | Jenkins (`Jenkinsfile`) | Runs on a dedicated Windows server. Builds Sneeze from source (15 deps, stamp-cached) because Sneeze CI doesn't produce Windows artifacts. Poll SCM trigger. |

**Why Jenkins for Windows?** Sneeze CI currently has no Windows build job. The GitHub Actions approach used by other platforms (download pre-built Sneeze libs as artifacts) doesn't work for Windows. A dedicated Jenkins server with persistent workspace lets Sneeze's 15-dep build (~60 min first time) stay stamp-cached across runs — subsequent builds only rebuild changed deps and recompile Rubidium.

**Jenkins setup:** Freestyle job with GitHub plugin for SCM polling. Single build step:
```
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File scripts\ci-windows.ps1
```
The `ci-windows.ps1` script clones/updates Sneeze, builds everything via the existing `build-windows.ps1` scripts, and generates the NSIS installer.

**Jenkins prerequisites:** Visual Studio 2022, CMake 3.24+, Git, Rust/Cargo, Python 3, Go, NASM, NSIS. The agent must run in a "Developer PowerShell for VS 2022" environment. Environment variable `SNEEZE_REPO_URL` must point to the Sneeze Git clone URL.

**Imported-target include-dir dualization is on-disk-conditional.** Rubidium's multi-config CMake tree (`CMAKE_CONFIGURATION_TYPES = Debug;Release`) plus the SDL3 imported-target dualization in `_rubidium_dualize_target` would, if applied unconditionally, rewrite `INTERFACE_INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES` to `…/$<LOWER_CASE:$<CONFIG>>/libs/SDL3/install/include`. CMake validates that genex per entry in `CMAKE_CONFIGURATION_TYPES` at generate time — so a tree with only one config's deps on disk would fail with `Imported target "SDL3::SDL3-static" includes non-existent path …/debug/libs/SDL3/install/include`. `_rubidium_dualize_target` therefore checks both `/debug/…` and `/release/…` resolutions of each include path on disk, and only emits the genex when both exist. When only one exists (e.g. Jenkins built only Release), the include path stays concrete to whatever `find_package` just resolved, which is always valid because the headers are config-invariant for the deps Rubidium ships. Library locations (`IMPORTED_LOCATION_*`, `IMPORTED_IMPLIB_*`) are still dualized unconditionally because CMake validates those lazily — at link time, per config built, not at generate. The Sneeze workspace on Jenkins agents typically has both deps configs because Sneeze's CI builds both; that's why Sneeze's transitively-imported targets don't trip the same generate-time validation.

### Hand-Maintained MSVC Projects (Windows Primary Dev Workflow)

On Windows, the Visual Studio solution is the **primary development artifact** — hand-maintained `.sln` and `.vcxproj` files live in `msvc/` and are checked into Git. This addresses two pain points: (1) adding files in CMake requires editing `CMakeLists.txt` and reconfiguring, while VS adds them to the `.vcxproj` automatically; (2) CMake-generated solutions are unstable (regenerate on every configure, breaking open VS instances).

**Architecture:**
- `Rubidium/msvc/Rubidium.sln` — references three projects: `Rubidium.vcxproj` (local), `../../Sneeze/msvc/Sneeze.vcxproj` (cross-repo), `../../Sneeze/msvc/SneezeTest.vcxproj` (cross-repo). Both Debug|x64 and Release|x64 configurations. Rubidium depends on Sneeze; SneezeTest depends on Sneeze.
- `Rubidium/msvc/Rubidium.vcxproj` — hand-maintained project for the Rubidium executable. Uses MSBuild property macros for portable paths: `$(RubidiumRoot)` = `$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\..`, `$(SneezeRoot)` = `$(RubidiumRoot)\..\..\Sneeze`, `$(SneezeDepsRoot)` = `$(SneezeRoot)\deps\builds\windows-x64`, `$(RubidiumDepsRoot)` = `$(RubidiumRoot)\deps\builds\windows-x64`, `$(CfgLower)` = `$([System.String]::new('$(Configuration)').ToLower())`. Source files use relative paths from `msvc/` (e.g., `../src/shell/App.cpp`). Include directories, preprocessor definitions, link libraries, and post-build DLL copies all use the macros. Output goes to `$(RubidiumBuildRoot)\install\$(CfgLower)\bin\`.
- `Rubidium/msvc/Rubidium.vcxproj.filters` — mirrors `src/` directory structure (shell, canvas, rmlui_sdl, inspector, inspector/network, logger, settings, updater, astro, pkg).
- `Sneeze/msvc/Sneeze.vcxproj` — hand-maintained project for the Sneeze static library. Uses `$(SneezeRoot)`, `$(SneezeDepsRoot)`, `$(CfgLower)` macros.
- `Sneeze/msvc/SneezeTest.vcxproj` — hand-maintained project for Sneeze's test suite (console app). Links `Sneeze.lib` + all Sneeze deps. Post-build copies `wasmtime.dll`.
- `.vcxproj.user` files are gitignored (personal debug settings like working directory).

**Precompiled headers:**
- The hand-maintained `.vcxproj` uses a traditional `pch.cpp` / `pch.h` pair (`<PrecompiledHeader>Create</PrecompiledHeader>` on `pch.cpp`). This file exists at `src/pch.cpp` and is checked in.
- CMake's `target_precompile_headers` generates its own separate `cmake_pch.cxx`. The two approaches don't conflict — CMake ignores `pch.cpp`, and the hand-maintained project ignores `cmake_pch.cxx`.
- Sneeze has the same pattern: `src/sneeze/Pch.cpp` for the hand-maintained project, CMake generates its own PCH source.

**Version header generation:**
- CMake generates `version.h` from `src/version.h.in` via `configure_file()`.
- The hand-maintained project generates `version.h` via a PowerShell `PreBuildEvent` that reads `VERSION` and substitutes into `version.h.in`, outputting to `$(IntDir)version.h`. The `$(IntDir)` is added to `AdditionalIncludeDirectories` for both `ClCompile` and `ResourceCompile` so the compiler and RC compiler can find it.

**Sync workflow (AI-driven):**
- The `.vcxproj` is the source of truth for source file lists on Windows. When files are added or removed in Visual Studio, they are automatically reflected in the `.vcxproj`.
- The `sync-build` skill (`.cursor/skills/sync-build.md`) synchronizes the hand-maintained `.vcxproj` with `src/CMakeLists.txt`. Invoked by saying "sync build" or "sync cmake".
- Procedure: generates a reference `.vcxproj` from CMake, compares file lists (normalizing paths), categorizes differences, and updates `CMakeLists.txt` to match.
- Expected permanent divergence: `src/pch.cpp` appears only in the hand-maintained project (CMake doesn't need it).
- Sneeze has its own independent `sync-build.md` skill with the same pattern.

**Coexistence with CMake:**
- The CMake build (`scripts/build-windows.ps1`) continues to work independently — it generates its own `.sln` under `builds/windows-x64/build/`. That generated solution is gitignored.
- The hand-maintained `msvc/Rubidium.sln` is what developers open day-to-day on Windows. The CMake-generated solution is used by CI and cross-platform builds.
- Both workflows produce output in the same locations (`builds/windows-x64/install/<config>/bin/`).

### Known Gotchas

- **Sneeze must be built first.** Rubidium's `add_subdirectory` needs Sneeze's installed dependencies at `Sneeze/deps/builds/<platform>/<config>/libs/`. Run Sneeze's build script before the first Rubidium build.
- **SDL pixel format must match ANARI byte layout.** ANARI's `ANARI_UFIXED8_RGBA_SRGB` writes R,G,B,A bytes. On little-endian Windows, `SDL_PIXELFORMAT_ABGR8888` matches. If colors look wrong (everything tinted blue/red), the pixel format is the first thing to check.
- **DLL copying.** The post-build step copies `wasmtime.dll` and `anari_library_halogen.dll` from `SNEEZE_LIBS_DIR`. SDL3 is now static and anari core is static. If the application fails to start with "DLL not found", verify the post-build step ran and the DLLs exist in the Sneeze deps install tree.
- **Two separate build trees.** `deps/builds/` holds SDL3. `builds/` holds Rubidium output. They are completely independent. Deleting `builds/<platform>/` for a clean Rubidium rebuild does not re-clone SDL3. Source clones in `deps/repos/` are shared across configs and survive any `builds/` cleanup. `-Rebuild` is gentler than a directory wipe: it runs `cmake --build --target clean --config <cfg>` to drop only the current config's compiled artifacts, leaving `CMakeCache.txt` + the generated `.sln`/`.vcxproj` intact (so an open Visual Studio solution stays valid), then wipes `install/<cfg>/`. The other config's `install/<other>/` is preserved.
- **Debug/Release separation (deps only).** The **deps** tree is per-config: `deps/builds/<plat>/debug/` vs `deps/builds/<plat>/release/` — never mix them (`_ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL` mismatch on MSVC will produce link errors). The **Rubidium** tree at `builds/<plat>/build/` is now a single multi-config CMake tree (VS/Xcode/Ninja-MC) that emits into `install/debug/` or `install/release/` depending on the config selected via the IDE dropdown or `cmake --build --config`. Both configs build against their respective per-config deps tree — both deps trees must exist on disk for both dropdown options to work.
- **Multi-config generator expressions.** Paths that used to be pinned per configure (`${SNEEZE_LIBS_DIR}/...`, imported-target `IMPORTED_LOCATION`) are now built from base paths + `$<LOWER_CASE:$<CONFIG>>` so they resolve at build time. Sneeze's `src/CMakeLists.txt` (path generalization block) rewrites both scalar path variables and transitive imported-target properties across `anari::anari_static` + `vox::vox`; Rubidium's `src/CMakeLists.txt` does the same for SDL3. The rewriter has a `_resolve_variant` helper that probes disk for Debug-suffix filename variants (`libcurl-d.lib` vs `libcurl.lib`, `openxr_loaderd.lib`, `spirv-cross-cored.lib`) because simple path substitution doesn't cover those name changes.
- **Debug library name suffixes (Sneeze-side).** Several Sneeze deps append `d` to library names in Debug builds: `openxr_loaderd.lib`, `libcurl-d.lib`, `spirv-cross-cored.lib` / `spirv-cross-glsld.lib` / `spirv-cross-hlsld.lib`. Sneeze's find modules (`FindSneezeOpenXR.cmake`, `FindSneezeCurl.cmake`) and Vox's `vox-config.cmake.in` list both Release and Debug names. If a new dep exhibits the same pattern, add both name variants to its find module.
- **Inspector toolbar controls disappear on resize if z-order is wrong.** The toolbar and filter bar use `STATIC` background windows for painting. Without `WS_CLIPSIBLINGS` on those STATICs, they paint over sibling controls (combobox, checkbox) during resize. Fix: `WS_CLIPSIBLINGS` on the background STATICs + `SetWindowPos(HWND_TOP)` for the combobox and checkbox at the end of `LayoutChildren`.
- **Inspector `WM_GETMINMAXINFO` arrives before `WM_CREATE`.** The `pImpl` pointer is NULL when this message fires, so it must be handled unconditionally before the `if (!pImpl)` guard in the WndProc.
- **ListView virtual mode (`LVS_OWNERDATA`) and owner-drawn buttons (`BS_OWNERDRAW`) have focus quirks.** Owner-drawn buttons retain `ODS_FOCUS` after click, leaving a persistent highlight. Fix: `SetFocus(hWnd)` after each button click to yank focus back to the parent, plus custom subclass (`ToolbarBtnSubclass`) for explicit hover tracking via `TrackMouseEvent`.
- **Sneeze `CACHE::MANAGER::Files()` (formerly `GetHistory()`) renamed in Sneeze as of 2026-05-01.** Rubidium `Tab_Network.cpp` must use `Files()` not `GetHistory()`.
- **Sneeze `CACHE::FILE` snapshot/attach lifecycle (as of 2026-05-02).** FILE objects carry snapshot fields (URL, state, size, content-type, timing, HTTP status, etc.) that survive `Release()`. After release, `m_pEntry` is null and the ENTRY may be evicted from memory, but the FILE's snapshot fields remain valid for list view display. To access ENTRY-dependent data (headers map, response body, disk path, access stats), call `pFile->Request()` to reattach — returns `true` on success, `false` if the entry was re-fetched (nEntryIx mismatch). Call `pFile->Release()` when done. The inspector's detail pane uses this: `ShowDetail` calls `Request()`, `HideDetail` calls `Release()`. The list view never needs to attach — snapshot fields are always available.
- **Detail pane must use a custom window class, not SS_OWNERDRAW STATIC.** `SS_OWNERDRAW` STATIC controls do not receive `WM_LBUTTONDOWN` messages in their subclass — they send `STN_CLICKED` to their parent. The detail panel registers `RubidiumDetailPanel` as a `WNDCLASSEXW` with its own `DetailPanelWndProc` to handle tab clicks, close button, and scroll events directly.
- **`CACHE::FILE::FetchDuration()` returns seconds, not milliseconds.** `FormatDuration()` expects milliseconds. Always multiply by 1000.0 before passing to `FormatDuration`. Cache-hit files have `FetchStartTime() == 0.0` and `FetchDuration() == 0.0` — display as `(cache)` in the Time column, not `pending`.
- **Windows Terminal ignores legacy console APIs.** On Windows 11, `SetCurrentConsoleFontEx` and `SetConsoleWindowInfo` have no effect when the default terminal is Windows Terminal. Console font/size customization requires Windows Terminal's own settings (JSON profiles) or is deferred until Rubidium has its own GUI. Don't waste time on `SetConsoleFont` calls.
- **nlohmann/json default-constructed objects are `null`, not `{}`.** `nlohmann::json j;` creates a null value. Calling `.value()` on null throws `json::type_error`. Always initialize to `nlohmann::json::object()` when the code expects a JSON object (e.g., `LoadUpdatesJson`). This was a silent crash that caused `RubidiumSetup.exe --check` to appear to do nothing on first run.
- **DWM frame extension must match title bar height.** `DwmExtendFrameIntoClientArea` top margin tells the DWM where its caption buttons go. If the margin doesn't match the custom title bar height, DWM buttons appear misaligned (gap below or above). Always pass `pImpl->nTitleBarHeight` as the top margin.
- **Title bar height: sum then scale.** Compute as `SM_CYCAPTION + 2 * SM_CYBORDER`, then DPI-scale the sum once via `MulDiv(sum, dpi, 96)`. Scaling each metric individually and summing loses fractional pixels, making the title bar 1-2px too short.
- **Child window subclassing for resize grips.** The viewport STATIC and its SDL child cover the frame edges, eating `WM_NCHITTEST` for resize. Both must be subclassed to return `HTTRANSPARENT` near the parent's border region. The SDL child is created by SDL *after* `APPFRAME::Initialize`, so subclassing must happen in `PollEvents` (checked each frame until child appears, then `bChildrenSubclassed` flag prevents repeat).
- **Rendering pipeline: native surface rendering at 60 FPS (updated 2026-04-29).** Halogen/Filament renders directly to the platform window's Vulkan swapchain via the `HALOGEN_NATIVE_SURFACE` ANARI extension — zero CPU copies, zero readback. The compositor passes the native window handle (HWND on Windows) from `CANVAS::NativeWindowHandle()` to `RENDERER::SetNativeWindow()` before initialization. When `IsRenderingToNativeSurface()` is true, the publish block is skipped entirely (no `GetFrameBuffer`, `WriteFrameBuffer`, `OnFrameReady`, no SDL texture upload). Filament owns presentation and vsync via its internal Vulkan swapchain. FPS telemetry: `frame ~16.8ms | submit ~0.1ms | render ~16.7ms` — the "render" time is dominated by vsync wait (`anariRenderFrame` + `anariFrameReady`), actual GPU work is sub-millisecond for the current solar system demo. The old GPU→CPU→GPU readback path (30 FPS, three full-frame memcpys) is still available as a fallback when `HALOGEN_NATIVE_SURFACE` is not supported or the renderer doesn't set a native window. Resize is handled by `ANARI_RENDERER::Resize()` which recreates the native surface object at the new dimensions (Filament's swapchain recreates automatically). Minor visual flicker during fast resize drags is expected — Filament briefly destroys and recreates the Vulkan swapchain, causing 1-2 frames of DWM background to show through.
- **Multi-window shutdown ordering.** Each `APPFRAME_NATIVE` window receives `WM_CLOSE` → `WM_DESTROY`. On `WM_DESTROY`, the window calls `m_pController->Window_OnDestroy(this)`, which removes it from `APP::m_apAppFrame`, saves window geometry (only on last-window-close), deletes the `APPFRAME_NATIVE`, and calls `PostQuitMessage(0)` when the vector is empty. The message loop in `APP::PollEvents` then exits, `Sneeze.Shutdown()` runs (tears down Filament/Vulkan while the process is still alive), and `APP::Run()` returns. `PostQuitMessage` is NEVER called from `APPFRAME` — only the controller decides when to quit. If `DestroyWindow` is called in `WM_CLOSE` (the traditional pattern), Filament's deferred commit buffer flushes during `anariRelease(device)` and attempts `vkCreateSwapchainKHR` on the dead HWND, producing `vkCreateSwapchainKHR failed. error=-1`.
- **`SetSneeze` timing is critical.** `pCanvas.SetSneeze(&pSneeze)` must be called AFTER `pSneeze.Initialize()` completes. If called before `pAppFrame.Initialize()`, `WM_SIZE` fires during window creation, `Canvas::Resize` forwards to `Sneeze::Resize` before Sneeze is initialized, queueing a stale pending resize. When Sneeze later starts, it immediately applies the stale size (different from the dimensions passed to `Initialize`), causing a framebuffer/texture dimension mismatch that makes every frame get dropped. Diagnosed 2026-04-28 via instrumented logging.
- **Resize pipeline: AppFrame → Canvas → Sneeze → Compositor.** `onSize` (Win32) calls `Canvas::Resize` which holds `m_textureMutex`, recreates the SDL texture, calls `SDL_SetWindowSize`, and forwards to `Sneeze::Resize`. Sneeze queues the resize (mutex-protected pending flag). The compositor thread calls `ConsumePendingResize` each frame and calls `RENDERER::Resize` (recreates ANARI frame, NOT full device teardown). `Canvas::Present` also holds `m_textureMutex` and drops frames where incoming dimensions don't match texture dimensions. This prevents the `SDL_UpdateTexture` buffer overrun that was crashing the app during vertical resizes (confirmed via debugger — `memmove`/`memcpy` overwrite).
- **Native surface rendering Y-axis orientation.** Halogen's native surface path (`HALOGEN_NATIVE_SURFACE`) produces astronomically correct Y orientation — verified against JPL Horizons ephemeris data for Mercury on 2026-04-30. The old readback path (pre-native-surface, `anariMapFrame` → `memcpy` → SDL texture) produced a Y-flipped image — Mercury appeared above the ecliptic when it should have been below. The flip was caused by the readback buffer having bottom-to-top row order (OpenGL convention) being copied directly to an SDL texture that expects top-to-bottom. The native surface path bypasses readback entirely; Halogen presents directly to the Vulkan/D3D swapchain with correct orientation. **Current status of the readback fallback:** forcing the readback path (by not calling `SetNativeWindow` on the renderer) produces a white/blank SDL window — Halogen appears to require a native surface to produce output in the current build. The readback path is non-functional with the current rendering architecture (compositor thread drives rendering). This is a known gap but not urgent — native surface is the primary and only active rendering path.
- **RmlUi Inspector CSS layout conventions (established 2026-05-10, updated 2026-05-29).** All control bars (tab bar, toolbar, status bar, list headers, container header) use a consistent pattern: `height: 26dp` (tab bar is 27dp), `padding: 3-5dp`, `box-sizing: border-box`, with `vertical-align: middle` on inline-block children for text centering. **DPI scaling (completed 2026-05-29):** uses RmlUi's native `dp` unit for all layout dimensions (fonts, padding, heights, widths) — authored at 1x logical scale, automatically multiplied by `Rml::Context::SetDensityIndependentPixelRatio(SDL_GetWindowDisplayScale())` at layout time. Hairline borders use `1px` (always exactly 1 physical pixel regardless of scale). Active-tab accent borders use `2dp` (scale with content). The `{{FONT_NN}}` template placeholder system has been fully removed — no C++ string manipulation for sizing. Font family is injected into the CSS string via `[{FONT-FAMILY}]` token replacement BEFORE `LoadDocument()` — this ensures `font-family` is present during CSS parsing and avoids the "No font face defined" warnings that occur when setting it programmatically after load. The replacement uses `APPNATIVE::GetInstance()->sFontFamily()` (resolved at startup from platform-specific font APIs). `SDL_WINDOW_HIGH_PIXEL_DENSITY` flag is set on window creation. Render scale set to `SDL_GetWindowPixelDensity()` (handles macOS Retina). `RmlUi_Platform_SDL`'s `GetPixelDensity` lambda returns `SDL_GetWindowPixelDensity(target_window)` for correct mouse coordinate translation. Display scale changes are handled in real-time via `SDL_EVENT_WINDOW_DISPLAY_SCALE_CHANGED` → `SetDensityIndependentPixelRatio`. Separator lines are exactly 1px `#D4E3FD` everywhere — `border-bottom-width: 1px` on the bar itself, never doubled. When an element's children are inline-block with their own padding, the parent's padding is set to `0` and children handle their own spacing (avoids double-padding that causes text misalignment). Column separators in the file list are full-height `position: absolute` divs with cumulative `left` percentages (not `border-right` on cells, which only extends the cell height). `overflow: hidden` on parent containers, `overflow: auto` on scrollable child areas. Font weight `bold` on tab bar only — all other text uses normal weight. **Nomenclature:** tab buttons use `id="tab-<name>"`, content areas use `id="panel-<name>"`, CSS classes are `.tab` and `.panel`. The word "widget" refers to a self-contained inspector sub-component (toolbar, statusbar, file list, etc.) that defines its own RML fragment and injects via `Element::SetInnerRML()`.
- **Release PDBs with optimizations preserved (MSVC).** Both Rubidium's `src/CMakeLists.txt` and Sneeze's `src/CMakeLists.txt` enable PDB generation for Release builds via an `if (MSVC)` block placed **after** `project()` and **before** `add_subdirectory(Sneeze)` (Rubidium-side) / before any target definition (Sneeze-side). The flags are `/Zi` at compile (emits debug info into `.obj`) + `/DEBUG` at link (tells the linker to produce a `.pdb` next to the binary) + `/OPT:REF` + `/OPT:ICF` at link (force-re-enabled — `/DEBUG` alone silently disables those and would leave dead code and identical-function duplicates in the binary). Applied via `add_compile_options` / `add_link_options` with `$<CONFIG:Release>` generator expressions. PDBs land next to each `.exe` / `.lib`. Binary size and codegen are identical to a no-PDB Release build. The Rubidium-side block propagates into Sneeze's targets when Sneeze is built inline, so both ends get PDBs from a single Rubidium build.
- **SDL event dispatch: single centralized poll loop (established 2026-05-29).** SDL events are polled exactly once per frame in `APPNATIVE::Impl::EventLoop()` via `while (SDL_PollEvent(&Event)) m_pApp->SDLWindow_Translate(Event)`. The `SDLWindow_Translate` method (in `APP` base class) extracts `windowID` from the event's union member (switch on event type), does an O(1) unordered_map lookup, and calls `HandleEvent()` on the matching `ISDLWINDOW`. **Never add a second `SDL_PollEvent` loop** — events consumed by one loop are invisible to others, causing dropped input. If a new SDL window is created, it must implement `ISDLWINDOW` and register via `APP::SDLWindow_Register()`. **ProcessInput vs HandleEvent:** `HandleEvent` updates raw input state (called per-event, may fire multiple times per frame); `ProcessInput` feeds accumulated deltas to the engine (called exactly once per frame per AppFrame). RmlUi windows call `Render()` at the end of `HandleEvent` for immediate repaint; CANVAS accumulates until `ProcessInput`. `ProcessInput` is called for ALL AppFrames, not just the active one — otherwise scroll deltas accumulated on unfocused windows are never consumed.
- **RmlUi inspector rendering is event-driven, not polled.** `RMLUI_SDL::Render()` (context Update + Render) is called from `HandleEvent()` after each SDL event is processed (immediate repaint on interaction) and from `Toggle()` when showing the window. It is NOT called from any per-frame loop or timer. This means the inspector only repaints when it receives SDL input. **Future requirement:** when Sneeze cache events or console logs arrive asynchronously, they will need to trigger a `Render()` call — likely via `PostMessage` to wake `MsgWaitForMultipleObjects` followed by a dirty-flag-driven render. This is a known gap; rendering from data callbacks is not yet implemented.
- **SDL3 `SDL_EVENT_WINDOW_MOVED` / `SDL_EVENT_WINDOW_RESIZED` fire once upon completion.** These are NOT continuous (unlike Win32's `WM_MOVE`/`WM_SIZE` which fire inside the modal drag loop). They fire a single time when the user finishes the interactive move or resize gesture. Safe to use for persistence without debouncing.
- **Inspector window geometry persistence pattern.** `RMLUI_SDL` exposes `FN_WINDOW_GEOMETRY` callback (typedef: `void (*)(int nX, int nY, int nWidth, int nHeight, void* pUserData)`) + `SetGeometryCallback()`. `INSPECTOR_RML::Impl` registers a static `OnGeometryChanged` that writes to `settings.json["inspector_rml"]`. On init, reads stored x/y/width/height from settings (empty object = defaults), passes size to `Initialize()`, calls `SetPosition()` if stored position is valid (>= 0). Different from the main app window pattern (which only saves on last-window-close) — the inspector saves geometry on every completed move/resize, since there's only ever one inspector window and losing position on crash would be annoying.

### Sneeze Inline-Build Isolation (Resolved 2026-04-21)

All Sneeze build activity — intermediates (`.vcxproj`, `.obj`) and final outputs (`Sneeze.lib`, test/tool exes) — now stays entirely within `Sneeze/builds/<plat>/`. Nothing Sneeze-related is written under Rubidium's build tree (except a path reference inside `Rubidium.sln` pointing at the Sneeze `.vcxproj`).

Two changes in `src/CMakeLists.txt` accomplished this:

1. `set (SNEEZE_BUILD_ROOT "${SNEEZE_DIR}/builds/${RUBIDIUM_PLATFORM}")` — routes final outputs (`Sneeze.lib` → `Sneeze/builds/<plat>/install/<cfg>/lib/`, test/tool exes → `.../bin/`).
2. `add_subdirectory ("${SNEEZE_DIR}/src" "${SNEEZE_DIR}/builds/${RUBIDIUM_PLATFORM}/build")` — routes all intermediates (`.vcxproj`, `.obj`, `.pch`, SPIR-V outputs) into `Sneeze/builds/<plat>/build/`.

This is the same `build/` directory that Sneeze's standalone build uses. Standalone and inline builds overwrite each other at that location, which is acceptable — you're either building from Rubidium or from Sneeze standalone, not both simultaneously. If you switch between them, a reconfigure sorts it out.

A third change — adding `CMAKE_ARCHIVE_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY` alongside the existing `CMAKE_RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY` / `CMAKE_LIBRARY_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY` settings in Rubidium's output dir block — eliminated stray empty `Debug/` and `Release/` folders that Visual Studio was creating under `Rubidium/builds/<plat>/build/` for any output type not explicitly redirected.

**Important:** after changing `add_subdirectory`'s binary dir path, a fresh configure (`-Fresh`) is required. `-Rebuild` alone reuses the stale CMake cache and produces `_ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL` mismatch link errors because the old cached paths still point at the previous binary dir.

### Versioning

Both Rubidium and Sneeze use semantic versioning (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH). The version lives in a `VERSION` file at each repo root. Current Rubidium version: **0.0.1**. CMake reads this file at configure time via `file(STRINGS)` and uses it for `project(VERSION ...)`, compile definitions (`RUBIDIUM_VERSION`, `SNEEZE_VERSION`), and CPack naming.

The `src/CMakeLists.txt` uses `set_property(DIRECTORY APPEND PROPERTY CMAKE_CONFIGURE_DEPENDS "${CMAKE_SOURCE_DIR}/VERSION")` so that editing the `VERSION` file automatically triggers CMake re-configuration on next build — no manual `cmake -S . -B build` step needed. Just edit `VERSION` and build.

**Version resource file (`src/rubidium.rc.in`, `pkg/setup.rc.in`):** Both Rubidium.exe and RubidiumSetup.exe embed a Windows `VERSIONINFO` resource with `FileVersion`, `ProductVersion`, `CompanyName` (Metaversal Corporation), `FileDescription`, `LegalCopyright`, and `ProductName`. CMake generates the `.rc` from `.rc.in` templates using `configure_file()`, substituting `PROJECT_VERSION_MAJOR/MINOR/PATCH`.

### Installer and Auto-Updater

Rubidium includes a CPack-based installer and a built-in auto-update module.

**Installer (CPack):**
- Windows: NSIS installer (`Rubidium-<version>-windows-x64.exe`). Installs to `Program Files\Metaversal\Rubidium`, creates Start Menu and Desktop shortcuts, supports uninstall via Add/Remove Programs. Uses `pkg/logo-rounded.ico` for installer and uninstaller icons. Displays `pkg/LICENSE.md` (full terms from `rp1.com/terms`, ASCII-encoded — no smart quotes or Unicode dashes, NSIS can't render them) on the license page.
- **Installer filename pattern**: `Rubidium-<version>-<platform>.<ext>` where `<platform>` is the same `RUBIDIUM_PLATFORM` slug used everywhere else (`windows-x64`, `linux-x64`, `macos-arm64`, etc.). Controlled by `CPACK_PACKAGE_FILE_NAME` in `src/CMakeLists.txt` — without that override, CPack defaults to `CPACK_SYSTEM_NAME` (e.g. `win64`) and the `package_register` target (which builds its filename from `RUBIDIUM_PLATFORM`) would point at a non-existent file. Historical entries in `pkg/manifest.json` with `-win64.exe` URLs (versions 0.0.0 and 0.0.1) predate the override and are preserved as-is.
- macOS: DMG drag-and-drop image.
- Linux: TGZ archive.
- Output lands in `builds/<platform>/install/<config>/pkg/` — chosen per-config by a generated `CPackProjectConfig.cmake` that reads `CPACK_BUILD_CONFIG` at CPack time. CPack does not evaluate CMake generator expressions directly, so the per-config directory has to come from a script that runs at CPack time.
- Generate with: `cpack --config builds/windows-x64/build/CPackConfig.cmake -C Release` (requires NSIS installed on Windows). Run twice — once `-C Debug`, once `-C Release` — to populate both per-config `pkg/` siblings without touching the other.
- `CPACK_NSIS_DISPLAY_NAME` is `"Rubidium"` (no version suffix — Windows shows just "Rubidium" in Apps & features, matching how other applications appear). `CPACK_NSIS_INSTALLED_ICON_NAME` points to `bin\Rubidium.exe` so Windows displays the embedded icon in the app list.
- `CPACK_PACKAGE_INSTALL_DIRECTORY` is `"Rubidium"` (not the default which would produce a double `Metaversal\Metaversal` path since `CPACK_NSIS_INSTALL_ROOT` already includes `Metaversal`).

**Application Icon:**
- `pkg/logo-rounded.ico` — multi-resolution (16-256px), 32-bit PNG-compressed. Embedded in the executable via `src/rubidium.rc`. Generated via [redketchup.io/icon-converter](https://redketchup.io/icon-converter).
- Three variants exist: `logo-square.ico`, `logo-rounded.ico` (primary), `logo-round.ico`.

**Release Manifest (`pkg/manifest.json`):**
- Cumulative JSON file checked into the repo and uploaded to the CDN.
- Structure: `current` (channel index for fast client lookup) + `releases` (nested object keyed by platform → version for O(1) lookup).
- Example: `releases["windows-x64"]["0.0.5"]` → `{ "date", "url", "sha256", "notes" }`. No `platform` or `version` fields inside the entry — both are already the keys.
- Client lookup: `current["stable"]["windows-x64"]` → version string → `releases["windows-x64"][version]` → direct hit. No array scanning.
- Managed by the `GenerateManifest` build tool (`pkg/generate_manifest.cpp`) which has two commands:
  - `register` — computes SHA-256 of the installer, inserts a release entry at `releases[platform][version]` (no channel promotion)
  - `promote` — points `current[channel][platform]` at a registered version, verifying `releases[platform][version]` exists
- Convenience targets: `package_register` (register only) and `package_promote` (promote to stable).
- Channel assignment lives solely in the `current` index — release entries have no channel field.
- Uses standard 3-space JSON indentation (not Allman).
- Future consideration: as the manifest grows (9 platforms × many versions), may split into a slim `latest.json` (just current + matching entries) for the client and a full `manifest.json` archive for tooling/auditing.

**RubidiumSetup.exe (`pkg/RubidiumSetup.cpp`):**
- Standalone C++ executable that replaces the former NSIS stub installer and Rubidium's embedded update logic.
- Built alongside Rubidium as a CMake target (`RubidiumSetup`). Ships in `bin/` alongside `Rubidium.exe`.
- Also distributed as a standalone download on the website for fresh installs.
- Uses static `libcurl` from Sneeze's build output (no Sneeze.lib linkage), `nlohmann/json` (header-only), `bcrypt` (Windows system lib for SHA-256). Zero runtime DLLs.
- Win32 `WinMain` entry point — no console window.
- Three modes:
  - **Fresh install** (`RubidiumSetup.exe`, no args): Shows Win32 step-list dialog with four steps (Fetching release information, Downloading Rubidium x.x.x, Verifying download, Launching installer). Each step shows "..." when active and appends a green ✔ checkmark when complete. Progress bar tracks the download. Fetches manifest from CDN, downloads current stable installer, verifies SHA-256, launches package installer. Primary distribution artifact — users download this from the website.
  - **Check** (`RubidiumSetup.exe --check VERSION`): Silent background process. Respects 24-hour check interval (stored as `last_check` epoch seconds in `Updates.json`). Fetches manifest, compares versions, downloads newer installer to `%APPDATA%\Metaversal\Rubidium\Updates\`, verifies SHA-256, writes staged info to `Updates.json`. Writes operational log to `Updates/RubidiumSetup.log`.
  - **Apply** (`RubidiumSetup.exe --apply`): Reads `Updates.json`, launches staged package installer, cleans up. Uses non-throwing `fs::remove` (error_code overload) so `Updates.json` cleanup always executes even if the installer file is locked.
- `--force` flag (with `--check`): bypasses 24-hour interval, used by Ctrl+Shift+U "Check for Updates" in Rubidium.
- **Critical bug fix**: `LoadUpdatesJson()` must initialize `nlohmann::json jData = nlohmann::json::object()`, not `nlohmann::json jData;` (which produces `null`). Calling `.value()` on a null JSON throws `type_error`, silently crashing the process. This was the root cause of RubidiumSetup `--check` appearing to do nothing on first run.
- Replaces the former `pkg/stub_installer.nsi` — no NSIS plugins (NScurl, nsJSON) are needed anymore.

**Persisted State — Two Files, One Writer Each:**
- `%APPDATA%\Metaversal\Rubidium\Settings.json` — owned by the `APP` base class. Created on first launch with defaults; written back to disk at program exit (`~APP()` destructor). On Linux: `$HOME/.config/Metaversal/Rubidium/settings.json`. Contains: `home` (fabric URL, default `https://rp1.com/enter.msf`), `window` (`x`, `y`, `width`, `height`, `maximized` — saved from the last closed window, only written when the *last* window closes to avoid unnecessary writes), `updater.skipped_version` (string — skip applies only to that specific version, newer version triggers fresh prompt; auto-cleared when current version >= skipped version), `inspector` (`x`, `y`, `width`, `height`, `columns` — old Win32 inspector), `inspector_rml` (`x`, `y`, `width`, `height` — new RmlUi inspector, updated on every completed move/resize via `FN_WINDOW_GEOMETRY` callback). RubidiumSetup never writes to this file.
- `%APPDATA%\Metaversal\Rubidium\Updates\Updates.json` — owned by RubidiumSetup. Contains `last_check` (epoch seconds) and `staged` object (version, installer path, SHA-256, notes, date) when an update is ready. Rubidium reads but never writes (except `CleanupStaged` which removes the `staged` entry after skip/install).
- `%APPDATA%\Metaversal\Rubidium\Updates\RubidiumSetup.log` — operational log written by RubidiumSetup. Timestamped entries for check mode (current version, whether check was skipped/performed, manifest fetch result, version comparison, download/verify status, staged result) and apply/install modes. Useful for diagnosing update issues.

**Slimmed UPDATER class (`src/updater/Updater.h`, `src/updater/Updater.cpp`):**
- No HTTP client, no manifest parsing, no downloading, no SHA-256, no background threads. No direct file I/O for `Settings.json` — all settings access goes through `RUBIDIUM::SETTINGS::GetInstance()`.
- `CheckForStagedUpdate(sCurrentVersion)` — reads `Updates/Updates.json`, returns true if `staged` exists, installer file is present, AND staged version > current version. If staged version <= current (stale from a completed update), auto-cleans the staged entry and installer file.
- `IsVersionSkipped()` — compares staged version against `skipped_version` via `SETTINGS::JSON()`.
- `SpawnBackgroundCheck()` — launches `RubidiumSetup.exe --check VERSION` (detached, no window).
- `SpawnForceCheck(sCurrentVersion)` — clears `skipped_version` via `SETTINGS::JSON()`, launches `RubidiumSetup.exe --check VERSION --force`, sets `m_bWaitingForCheck` flag for poll-based result detection.
- `PollCheckResult()` — returns `CHECK_IDLE`, `CHECK_PENDING`, `CHECK_NO_UPDATE`, or `CHECK_UPDATE_AVAILABLE`. Monitors `Updates.json` for `last_check` timestamp >= spawn time to detect completion. Populates staged version/notes/installer when update is found.
- `SpawnApply()` — launches `RubidiumSetup.exe --apply`.
- `SkipVersion()` — writes `skipped_version` via `SETTINGS::JSON()`, deletes staged installer and clears `Updates.json`.
- `ClearStaleSkippedVersion()` — if `skipped_version` in settings is <= current version, clears it via `SETTINGS::JSON()`. Called on startup when no staged update is present.
- `CleanupStaged()` — removes staged installer file and `staged` entry from `Updates.json`.
- `UpdatesDir()` / `UpdatesJsonPath()` — derive paths from `SETTINGS::GetInstance()->sHomePath()`.
- Contains a private `CompareVersions()` helper (semantic version comparison, identical to the one in RubidiumSetup.cpp).
- `UPDATER` is an abstract base with a factory (`Create()`). Platform subclasses: `UPDATER_WND` (Win32, MessageBox prompt) and `UPDATER_GENERIC` (stub for non-Windows). The former `GetAppDataDir()` virtual has been removed — path resolution is now centralized in `SETTINGS`. Getter methods: `StagedVersion()`, `StagedNotes()`, `SetupExePath()` (virtual, per-platform).

**Startup Flow (`APP::Run()`):**
1. `SETTINGS` singleton is initialized on first access (resolves app data path, loads `settings.json` or creates with defaults).
2. Log level restored from `settings.json` via `m_pLogger->LogLevel()`.
3. Sneeze engine created and initialized (`SNEEZE Sneeze(this)` — `APP` is the `ISNEEZE` host).
4. Initial window created from persisted geometry (`settings.json` `window` key) via `Window_Create()`. If `maximized` was true, `ShowWindow(SW_MAXIMIZE)`.
5. Message loop entered via `PollEvents()`. Loop exits when last window closes.
6. `Sneeze.Shutdown()` after loop exit.

**Updater flow (currently commented out, pending re-integration with multi-window architecture):**
1. Check `Updates.json` for a staged update via `CheckForStagedUpdate(RUBIDIUM_VERSION)`. Also auto-cleans stale staged entries (staged version <= current).
2. If staged and version != `skipped_version`: prompt user with MessageBox (Yes/No/Cancel).
   - Yes: spawn `RubidiumSetup.exe --apply`, quit Rubidium.
   - No (Skip): write `skipped_version` to `Settings.json`, delete staged installer, continue.
   - Cancel: continue without action (re-prompts next launch).
3. If no staged update: clear stale `skipped_version` (if <= current version), spawn `RubidiumSetup.exe --check VERSION` in background, continue.

**In-App Update Check (Ctrl+Shift+U — currently commented out, pending re-integration):**
- `APPFRAME` handles Ctrl+Shift+U as an accelerator on the ellipsis menu's "Check for Updates" item.
- Update check triggers `SpawnForceCheck()` which clears `skipped_version`, launches `RubidiumSetup.exe --check VERSION --force`, sets `m_bWaitingForCheck`.
- Polls `PollCheckResult()` each frame. When RubidiumSetup finishes:
  - `CHECK_NO_UPDATE` → MessageBox "You are running the latest version."
  - `CHECK_UPDATE_AVAILABLE` → Same Yes/No/Cancel prompt as startup. Yes quits and applies; No skips.

**NSIS Finish Page:**
- `CPACK_NSIS_MUI_FINISHPAGE_RUN` is set to `Rubidium.exe`, adding a "Run Rubidium" checkbox (checked by default) on the installer's final page.

**`pkg/LICENSE.md` — Encoding Requirement:**
- Contains the full Metaversal Terms of Use from `rp1.com/terms`.
- **Must be ASCII-encoded.** NSIS's license page cannot render UTF-8 smart quotes (`\u201C`, `\u201D`, `\u2018`, `\u2019`) or em-dashes (`\u2014`). These render as garbage characters. All curly quotes must be replaced with straight ASCII quotes, em-dashes with `--`, en-dashes with `-`.
- If the terms are re-exported from the website, the ASCII conversion must be re-applied.

**CDN Configuration:**
- `RUBIDIUM_CDN_URL` — CMake cache variable, default `https://dean.rp1.dev:884/_rubidium/`. Compiled into the binary.
- `RUBIDIUM_PLATFORM` — auto-detected from OS and architecture (e.g., `windows-x64`, `macos-arm64`). Also a CMake cache variable.
- Manifest URL is `RUBIDIUM_CDN_URL + "manifest.json"`.


**Static linking decisions:**
- SDL3: statically linked (`SDL3::SDL3-static`). No `SDL3.dll` to distribute.
- ANARI core: statically linked (`anari::anari_static`). No `anari.dll` to distribute.
- Halogen/Filament device: remains dynamic (`anari_library_halogen.dll`) — the ANARI SDK builds device libraries as shared plugins by design. Located in `Sneeze/deps/builds/<platform>/<config>/libs/Halogen/install/bin/`. Sole ANARI device consumed by Rubidium (Helide retired — `BUILD_HELIDE_DEVICE=OFF` on both Sneeze's `anari-sdk` and `anari-sdk-release` ExternalProjects; helide's Embree sub-dep was also blowing out Windows `MAX_PATH` on longer checkout paths).
- Wasmtime: remains dynamic (`wasmtime.dll`) due to size (~23 MB) and potential CRT conflicts with static Rust runtime.

## Roadmap

Rubidium's roadmap tracks application-layer work. Engine-layer roadmap (SOM, SMAP, WASM services, OpenXR integration, etc.) lives in Sneeze's `project.mdc`.

